# Documentation Standards

## Purpose

Right-sized documentation — enough to onboard and maintain, not bureaucracy.

## When to Document

| Trigger | Action |
|---------|--------|
| New feature (user-visible) | Update README or feature doc |
| API change | Update OpenAPI / API.md |
| Architecture decision | ADR in `docs/adr/` |
| Env / setup change | Update README setup section |
| Internal refactor only | Code comments if non-obvious |

## Document Locations

```
README.md                 # overview, quickstart
docs/
  specs/                  # feature specifications
  adr/                    # architecture decision records
  guides/                 # how-to guides
  api/                    # OpenAPI, endpoint docs
ARCHITECTURE.md           # system overview (optional)
```

## Doc Structure (default)

1. **Context** — why this exists
2. **How it works** — behavior, flows
3. **How to use** — commands, examples
4. **Caveats** — limits, known issues

## Style

- Direct, pragmatic prose — complete sentences.
- Code examples must be runnable or clearly marked pseudo.
- Keep docs in sync with code in the same PR when possible.

## Templates

- Feature spec: `templates/feature-spec.template.md`
- Design doc: `templates/design-doc.template.md`
- ADR: `templates/adr.template.md`

## AI Behavior

- Suggest doc updates with ready-to-paste snippets.
- Do not create new doc files unless user requests or change is significant.